# Line-ending policy
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Line endings used to be inconsistent: most text files were stored with LF,
# but a handful (Makefile, rules/config.json, several frontend sources) were
# stored with CRLF. Since core.autocrlf normalizes CRLF to LF on `git add`,
# merely touching one of those files rewrote every line and buried the real
# change under thousands of phantom diff lines.
#
# Store every text file as LF in the repository, and check it out as LF too.
# Git still accepts CRLF in the working tree and normalizes it back to LF, so
# an editor that writes CRLF can no longer produce EOL-only diffs.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows scripts keep CRLF in the working tree (Windows tooling and legacy
# PowerShell hosts are unreliable with LF-only script files).
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Shell scripts must keep LF line endings: they are executed in WSL, in
# containers and on CI runners, where a CRLF shebang fails with
# "env: 'bash\r': No such file or directory".
*.sh text eol=lf

# Binary assets must never be line-ending converted.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary
*.dll   binary
*.exe   binary
*.syso  binary
*.pfx   binary
*.msix  binary
